This paper is concerned with an elliptic system of partial differential equations, consisting of the following two equations, where the unknown functions map to the Euclidean space: \begin{eqnarray}
\Delta f_i &=& \sum\limits_{j=1}^m f_j^2 g_i(x), \quad i=1,2,\\
f_1(x) &\ne& f_2(x) \text{ for some } x \in \mathbb{R}^2.
\end{eqnants}
We demonstrate that, in the absence of a radial assumption, no solutions exist when the domain is two-dimensional. Additionally, when the domain is higher-dimensional, we show that no solutions exist if the behavior of the function $f_i$ is such that it maps to a set with finite volume. This is a distinct contribution, as previous work required the symmetry of the solution and resulted in solutions consisting of a finite number of spheres for the corresponding scalar equation. The key aspect of this new result highlights the significant role of the symmetry of the solution in the case of the scalar equation, as demonstrated by the example of a conical structure of solutions. The corresponding results for the scalar equation consist of a single equation: \begin{eqnarray}
\Delta f &=& f^2 g(x), \quad f(x) \ne f(y) \text{ for some } x, y \in \mathbb{R}^2.
\end{eqnants}
We further show in the scalar case that no solutions exist in any dimensional space except for two dimensions, and we demonstrate this nonexistence by proving that, in one-dimensional space, no solutions exist, while in three-dimensional space, solutions consist of a set of disjoint lines. The approach used to achieve this result involves the application of an integral operator to the equation, which yields the image of the solution function $f$, resulting in the function $f$ mapping to a set with the property of being convex or concave along certain lines. This implies that the solution function $f$ must be constant, as we have shown that no solutions exist for the scalar equation in one-dimensional space. The extension of this result to three-dimensional space involves the use of an integral equation to show that the solution consists of a set of disjoint lines. The final aspect of the scalar case involves showing that solutions exist in two-dimensional space, and we achieve this by constructing counterexamples. The approach used in these constructions involves the application of
